      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "Our aim was to evaluate the hypotheses that women with severe angina and normal coronary arteries (syndrome X) have an increased incidence of hysterectomy and show a positive cardiac response to 17 beta-estradiol replacement therapy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
          "content": "The gynecologic histories of 30 women with syndrome X were determined. Anginal and 17 beta-estradiol insufficiency symptoms were recorded daily for 1 month. Subsequently, 20 of the women underwent hyperemic response testing before and after 2 months of estrogen replacement therapy. Hyperemic response results were compared with those of 12 asymptomatic post-menopausal women not receiving estrogen replacement therapy. Symptoms were recorded daily during estrogen replacement therapy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "A total of 18 women (60%) underwent hysterectomy. All were experiencing hot flushes. Hyperemic response was diminished in women with syndrome X compared with controls. Hyperemic response increased and anginal symptoms decreased during estrogen replacement therapy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The incidence of hysterectomy was increased and estrogen replacement therapy alleviated cardiac symptoms and enhanced hyperemic response in a group of women with syndrome X."
        }
      ]},
